#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
